  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "81da3d2d-ffb8-493a-9149-107dfd27bb01",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 351,
      "shortId": "JL4K4",
      "priority": 1,
      "shortName": "focusscope-a-container-s-subtr",
      "title": "FocusScope: a container's subtree takes keyboard events only while focused",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "# What\n\nMake the wrapper the host already puts around every mounted container the **keyboard gate**: pointer\nevents always enter; `Event::Key` and `Event::Widget` enter only while that container holds chrome\nfocus. This is what makes \"the keys go to the focused component\" implementable at all — without it the\nhost would have to know where each container sits in the retained tree.\n\n# Current state\n\n`FocusRing` (`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/focus_ring.rs`, landed F003/P011/T020) wraps every container in\n`focus_and_pick` (`heca/src/chrome/mod.rs`) and is bound to that mount's `keyboard_target` signal\n(`SharedChromeState::container_keyboard_target`). It only **paints** — it does not gate anything.\n\n`ScrollRegion::keyboard_target` (`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/scroll_region.rs:476`) gates scroll intents\nper region and stays useful for several scroll areas *inside* one container, but it is the wrong place\nfor the container-level gate (every future widget would need its own copy).\n\n# The shape\n\nThe routing contract is in `heca-grid-ui/src/component.rs:682` (`dispatch`): `on_event_capture` first,\nthen the child walk unless `routes_own_subtree()`, then `on_event`. A gate that returns\n`Handled::Yes` would stop the **sibling** walk, so the focused container would never be reached. The\nexpressible form is to own the walk:\n\n```rust\nfn routes_own_subtree(&self) -> bool { true }\nfn on_event_capture(&mut self, ev: &Event) -> Handled {\n    if matches!(ev, Event::Key { .. } | Event::Widget(..)) && !self.focused.get_untracked() {\n        return Handled::No;              // not mine — my siblings still get their turn\n    }\n    route_event(&mut self.base.children, ev)\n}\n```\n\n`route_event` is `pub(crate)`, which is another reason the gate belongs in the library beside the\nwrapper rather than app-side.\n\n# Steps\n\n1. Add the gate to `FocusRing` (renaming it — `FocusScope`? — is a judgement call: the ring is the\n   visible half of the same fact, so one widget with two jobs is honest, but the name should say\n   scope). Update the rustdoc, `docs/widgets.md` and the showcase entry.\n2. `tests/pointer_delivery.rs` **requires** anything claiming `routes_own_subtree` to still deliver\n   every pointer kind (press/move/release/wheel) to its children — add the case, and make it pass for\n   both focused and unfocused, because a click on an unfocused dock must still work (and should focus\n   it: see the mouse task).\n3. Tests: a widget intent reaches the focused scope's child and not an unfocused sibling's; an\n   unfocused scope does not consume it (the sibling after it still gets it); pointer events enter\n   regardless of focus.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- One gate, in the library, beside the ring it shares a signal with.\n- An unfocused container is inert to keys and fully alive to the mouse.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\n# ✅ DONE 2026-07-30 — commit `163f327`\n\n`FocusRing` renamed to **`FocusScope`** (`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/focus_scope.rs`) and given the\ngate. The name says scope because the widget now has two jobs from one signal, and a reader seeing\n\"ring\" would not expect it to swallow keys.\n\n```rust\nfn routes_own_subtree(&self) -> bool { true }\nfn on_event_capture(&mut self, ev: &Event) -> Handled {\n    if matches!(ev, Event::Key { .. } | Event::Widget(_)) && !self.focused.get_untracked() {\n        return Handled::No;          // not mine — my siblings still get their turn\n    }\n    route_event(&mut self.base.children, ev)\n}\n```\n\n**Why it has to own the walk**: swallowing (`Handled::Yes`) stops the walk at *this* node, so a\nsibling scope — the one that actually holds focus — would never be reached; declining without owning\nthe walk would let the event through anyway, since `dispatch` does the child walk itself\n(`component.rs:682`). So the scope takes the walk and skips it for the kinds that are not its own.\n`Event::ModifiersChanged` is deliberately **not** gated (a broadcast; widgets keep modifier state\nconsistent either way).\n\n## Tests\n\nSix in `focus_scope.rs`: an intent enters a focused scope; does not enter an unfocused one; **an\nunfocused scope declines so a focused sibling still gets its turn** (the load-bearing one — the inert\nscope is placed *first*, which is the arrangement that breaks if it consumes); a key is gated like an\nintent; every pointer kind reaches an unfocused scope; the gate follows the signal with no rebuild.\n\n`tests/pointer_delivery.rs` gained two cases (focused + **unfocused**) requiring the whole pointer set\nto reach the child — the unfocused one is the sharp case, since a click on a dock without focus is how\nyou focus it.\n\n## Renamed everywhere\n\n`heca-grid-ui/src/{lib.rs, widgets/mod.rs}`, `heca/src/chrome/mod.rs` (import, `focus_and_pick`, two\ntests), `heca-renderer/examples/showcase.rs`, `docs/widgets.md` (section + TOC + focus-model table +\nthe ScrollRegion note), `docs/chrome-and-ui.md` §2.10, `AGENTS.md` catalog. `PaintCx::focus_ring`\n(the paint primitive) keeps its name.\n\n## Consequence for the next tasks\n\nThe gate is what P085/T352 dispatches into: the host sends one `Event::Widget(..)` at the chrome root\nand only the focused container's subtree sees it. It also subsumes the container-level use of\n`ScrollRegion::keyboard_target` — that setter stays useful for several scroll areas *inside* one\ncontainer, but the container-level gate is here now.\n\n1017 tests, clippy clean.",
      "notes": "",
