    },
    {
      "id": "3555aed0-93d6-4b19-9e69-ba7ce2771657",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 356,
      "shortId": "F3J4W",
      "priority": 6,
      "shortName": "migrate-the-workspaces-contain",
      "title": "Migrate the workspaces container onto the contract; delete SidebarNav and the sidebar_* built-ins",
      "status": "in-progress",
      "description": "# What\n\nMake the first-party container the first consumer of the contract, and remove the bespoke mode it\nreplaces. This is the task that proves the design: if the workspace tree cannot be expressed as a\ncomponent that declares its actions, the design is wrong.\n\n# ✅ SETTLED — THE SIDEBAR TREE **IS** THE COMPONENT (user, 2026-07-30)\n\nRe-stated because a session asked it again and the task text did not answer it. **Do not re-open.**\n\n- **A component declares its actions** (→ `ActionRegistry`) **and their default keybindings**\n  (→ the keymap registry, user-configurable). That is the whole of its contract.\n- **It is up to the component to perform an action against its own focused item.** For\n  `WorkspacesContainerProvider` that item is a workspace, a column or a pane — *it* knows which,\n  because those are its rows.\n- **`SidebarTree` is that component's own domain state** — the analogue of `DockerService` in the\n  worked example. It lives on `AppState` today for historical reasons only.\n- **Therefore cursor movement is a DECLARED COMPONENT ACTION**, not a host facility. There is no\n  generic host-side \"step to the next `nav_key`\": a component like a hypothetical\n  *CurrentSelectedWorkspace* label declares **no** actions at all — it only reads state — and a\n  host-defined notion of \"next row\" would be meaningless to it.\n- `nav_key` (landed, F003/P085/T354) keeps its host-side job: **naming** a row so the cursor\n  highlight, the right-click target and later the drag can point at one. *Which* row is next is the\n  component's.\n- A component that declares nothing can still get a **context menu**: its entries may name existing\n  actions or its own.\n\n`ProviderCx::set_selected` (landed, F003/P085/T353) is how a component moves its own cursor — it\nwrites the per-mount cursor in the **store**, never `&mut AppState`.\n\n# ⚠️ THE PREREQUISITE THE TASK DID NOT DECLARE (found 2026-07-30)\n\n**Where the tree goes: `WorkspacesContainerState`** (`heca/src/chrome/state.rs:142`), *not* a field\non the provider struct. The reason is concrete: a provider is stored as `Box<dyn Provider>`, so\nanything app-side that wanted the tree back would need `as_any` + a downcast — host code reaching\ninto one specific component, which is the thing the pluggable design exists to prevent.\n`WorkspacesContainerState` already **is** this component's own state (`nav_selection`,\n`collapsed_ws`, `panes`, `selection`), it is already reachable from `ProviderCx::state().workspaces()`\nwith no downcast, and F003/P085/T360 already plans to key it by container. Put the tree there.\n\n**But it cannot move yet**, because the app still reads `AppState.sidebar_tree` at **46 sites across\n10 files**, and the biggest group is a *legacy fixed-row geometry* path that is still live:\n\n- `heca/src/sidebar/hit_test.rs` — `sidebar_hit_test` / `sidebar_button_hit_test`, row resolution by\n  arithmetic on row height;\n- `heca/src/mouse/surface_left.rs` (7 sites) and `heca/src/mouse/drag.rs` (3) — reached from\n  `mouse.rs:331` via `target::surface_click_action`, so it is **not** dead code;\n- `heca/src/chrome/mod.rs` — `pane_fallback_name` / `pane_custom_name` lookups, and the\n  `state.sidebar_tree.workspaces` walk at `:3644`.\n\n`nav_key` was built precisely to replace that: `nav::nav_key_at` resolves the topmost-deepest row\nunder a point from the **retained** tree's real laid-out bounds, the same walk `drag::resolve_at`\nuses. So **step 0 of this task is to delete the fixed-row path** and re-source the name lookups from\nthe store, after which the tree has one owner and one home.\n\nOrder that keeps the build green:\n\n0. Replace `sidebar_hit_test` / `sidebar_button_hit_test` with `nav_key_at` + the existing\n   `drag::resolve_at`; delete `heca/src/sidebar/hit_test.rs` and the `flat_items` / `button_hitboxes`\n   readers. Re-source `pane_fallback_name` / `pane_custom_name` from `WorkspacesContainerState`.\n1. Move `SidebarTree` into `WorkspacesContainerState`; delete `AppState.sidebar_tree`.\n2. Declare the actions + `perform`.\n3. Ship `[keys.workspaces]` defaults.\n4. Context menu → `container.workspaces` + `ContextTarget::Row`.\n5. Delete `SidebarNav` + the 11 `sidebar_*` built-ins.\n6. Docs + tests.\n\n# What is being deleted\n\n- `InputMode::SidebarNav` (`heca/src/app_state.rs:58`) + `handle_sidebar_nav_mode`\n  (`heca/src/app/input.rs`) + `restorable_mode` (`chrome/context_menu.rs:334`, whose only arm is\n  `SidebarNav`).\n- The 11 `sidebar_*` built-ins: `sidebar_focus`, `sidebar_up`, `sidebar_down`, `sidebar_left_nav`,\n  `sidebar_right_nav`, `sidebar_peek`, `sidebar_expand_toggle`, `sidebar_create_workspace`,\n  `sidebar_create_column`, `sidebar_split_in_column`, `sidebar_zoom_selected_column`,\n  `sidebar_delete_selected` — their `WmAction` variants, `action_from_name` arms, `action_priority`\n  arms, handlers (`handlers.rs:1562+`), registrations (`app/registry.rs`), descriptors, RPC commands\n  and the `[[keys.mode]] name = \"sidebar\"` block. **~140 references.**\n- The legacy fixed-row hit-testing above.\n- `state.chrome_state.workspaces.nav_selection` → the generic `container_cursor`, and with it the\n  single bridge `publish_sidebar_selection` → `selection_nav_key` that F003/P085/T354 left behind\n  **precisely so there is one place to delete here**.\n\n# What replaces it\n\n`WorkspacesContainerProvider` declares:\n\n- `kind() = \"workspaces\"` (bindings) vs `id()` = the mount (cursor, scroll, focus). **Landed** in\n  F003/P085/T353.\n- **Declared actions**, each acting on its own focused item: `workspaces.cursor_up` /\n  `workspaces.cursor_down`, `workspaces.activate_selected` (focus the pane/workspace under the\n  cursor — today's `sidebar_right_nav`/Enter), `workspaces.peek_selected` (focus but stay),\n  `workspaces.toggle_selected`, `workspaces.rename_selected`, `workspaces.delete_selected`,\n  `workspaces.create_workspace`, `workspaces.create_column`, `workspaces.split_selected`,\n  `workspaces.zoom_selected`. Each with label, icon, `policy`, and `confirm` where destructive.\n- **Bindings it does not declare**: anything already in the catalog (`zoom_column`, `close`,\n  `next_pane`) is simply **bound** in `[keys.workspaces]`, not redeclared.\n- `perform` resolves the cursor (`cx.selected()`) against its own tree and dispatches the existing\n  parameterized actions (`FocusPane`, `RenamePaneById`, `DeleteWorkspace`, …) through `cx.dispatch`.\n- Defaults shipped as `[keys.workspaces]` in `keybindings.default.toml`, preserving today's letters\n  (`j`/`k`/`h`/`l`/`Enter`/`Space`/`Tab`/`w`/`c`/`v`/`z`/`d`) so muscle memory survives.\n\n`sidebar_focus` (`prefix+e`) becomes: focus the navigable dock (already implemented, F003/P011/T020) —\nthe mode it used to enter no longer exists, because focus **is** the mode.\n\n# Behaviours to preserve (settled with /grill-me; do not casually re-decide)\n\nNavigation is selection-driven; the cursor does not move main focus; `h`/`l` are tree navigation on\nstructural rows; `Enter`/`l`/`Right` activate **and leave**, `Space` peeks **and stays**; `Esc` exits\nand focuses contextual content; mutation keys are container-only; the global collapse actions act on\nthe **active main-view** state, not the cursor.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- `prefix+Shift+e` → pick the dock → `j`/`k`/`Enter`/`d` work, unprefixed, exactly as `prefix+e` used to.\n- No `SidebarNav`, no `sidebar_*` action, no domain enum in the menu/nav path, no fixed-row geometry.\n- A component that declares **no** actions still works and can still carry a context menu.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings. **The USER drives it in the app.**",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [
        {
          "id": "3555aed0-93d6-4b19-9e69-ba7ce2771657-check-001-in-progress-blocked",
          "date": "2026-07-29T10:41:53.647Z",
          "fromStatus": "in-progress",
          "toStatus": "blocked",
          "title": "PARKED 2026-07-29 — blocked on P086's key plumbing. Steps 0/1/2 are done and step 2 is verified in the app; steps 3, 5, 6 are outstanding and step 4 has moved to P086/T365.",
          "description": "PARKED 2026-07-29 — blocked on P086's key plumbing. Steps 0/1/2 are done and step 2 is verified in the app; steps 3, 5, 6 are outstanding and step 4 has moved to P086/T365.\n\nWHY IT CANNOT PROCEED (verified in the code, not inferred): `keybindings.default.toml` ships NO `[keys.workspaces]` block. Every key the user verified — j/k/h/l/Space/Tab — comes from `ActionMeta.default_binding` via `bind_component_default` (heca/src/providers/actions.rs:57, re-applied on reload by `rebind_provider_defaults`). That field and that function are exactly what P086/T366 deletes, and P086/T362 replaces the config shape that must carry the keys instead.\n\nSo step 3 (move `sidebar_create_workspace`, `sidebar_create_column`, `sidebar_split_in_column`, `sidebar_zoom_selected_column`, `sidebar_delete_selected` into `actions()`/`perform()` as w/p/c/x) has no way to give those actions a key today that survives P086: either `default_binding` (deleted by T366) or a `[keys.workspaces]` block in the shape T362 rewrites. Either path lands unverifiable in the app and is then rewritten. The handler migration itself is NOT superseded — only its key half is.\n\nStep 5 (delete `SidebarNav` + the 11 `sidebar_*` built-ins) is blocked on step 3: deleting them removes w/c/v/z/d outright while the replacements have no keys. `sidebar_focus` additionally waits on P086/T363.\n\nUNBLOCKS WHEN: P086/T362 (`[[keys.component]]`) + P086/T366 (delete `default_binding`; keys come from the built keymaps) have landed, and P086/T363 has shipped `global_focus`/`Esc` for `sidebar_focus`'s retirement.\n\nDecided with the user 2026-07-29: T356 stays here and is finished after P086 rather than being folded into P086 — nothing is rewritten and the SidebarNav deletion keeps an owner."
        },
        {
          "id": "3555aed0-93d6-4b19-9e69-ba7ce2771657-check-002-blocked-in-progress",
          "date": "2026-07-29T13:38:21.562Z",
          "fromStatus": "blocked",
          "toStatus": "in-progress",
          "title": "UNPARKED 2026-07-29 — all three prerequisites landed: F003/P086/T362 (`[[keys.component]]`), F003/P086/T366 (`default_binding` deleted, keys come from the built keymaps), F003/P086/T363 (`global_focus` + `Esc`). Driven by F003/P086/T369.",
          "description": "UNPARKED 2026-07-29 — all three prerequisites landed: F003/P086/T362 (`[[keys.component]]`), F003/P086/T366 (`default_binding` deleted, keys come from the built keymaps), F003/P086/T363 (`global_focus` + `Esc`). Driven by F003/P086/T369.\n\nDECIDED WITH THE USER BEFORE STARTING\n1. **Which level owns w/p/c/x** — settled by P086's agreed model: keys are flat at the container level and the component decides meaning from the row the cursor is on. All are container-level declared actions resolving against the cursor.\n2. **The fifth built-in, `sidebar_zoom_selected_column`** — kept, not dropped. It zooms *the cursor's* column (switching workspace first if needed) where the global `zoom_column` zooms the active one, so it is genuinely selection-dependent.\n3. **The sixth, `delete_current_column` (`Shift+d`)** — not in the task text. It branches on `InputMode::SidebarNav` to delete the cursor's column; `x` cannot cover it because the cursor never lands on a column row (`is_navigable` stops on panes and workspaces).\n4. **The shape, chosen by the user: parameterized built-ins, not cursor-only handlers.**\n   - **ADD `zoom_column_at_index { ws_idx, col_idx }`** — genuinely missing; `WmAction::ZoomColumn` takes no args.\n   - **REUSE `delete_column`** — it is *already* delete-at-index (required `ws_idx` + `col_idx`, `heca/src/actions.rs:907`, `heca/src/input.rs:908`) and is what the sidebar column context menu already dispatches. **Not** renamed to `delete_column_at_index`: it is an existing action id, used by the menu and bindable, so a rename breaks configs for a naming nicety. **Its description is wrong and must be fixed** — it says \"Delete the focused column\" but deletes the one you name.\n   - The focused-target cases stay on `zoom_column` and `delete_current_column`.\n   - The component's `perform` resolves its cursor to `(ws_idx, col_idx)` and dispatches the indexed action, exactly as `activate_selected` already dispatches `focus_pane { pane_id }`. `delete_current_column` loses its `SidebarNav` branch.\n\nThis also makes both reachable from RPC and a menu with an explicit target, which is the win over cursor-only handlers."
        }
      ],
      "decisions": [],
