    {
      "id": "3b8117d2-e01d-4a89-84b9-641c33d3a50e",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 360,
      "shortId": "58SE7",
      "priority": 10,
      "shortName": "a-component-s-own-signals-a-pe",
      "title": "A component's own signals: a per-mount value store a plugin can write",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "# What\n\nGive a component a place to keep **state the retained UI tree binds to**, keyed by mount, that a\nWASM plugin can write across the boundary. Today there is none, so a plugin's only way to show a\nchanged value is to re-send its whole `ViewNode` tree and have the host rebuild it.\n\nRaised by the user 2026-07-30 while reading `heca/src/app/startup.rs:349` — *\"for augment I mean a\nplugin that wants to add a new item in the app state (a new signal)\"*.\n\n# Two kinds of state — only one of them is a gap\n\n- **A component's own domain state** (a Docker client, the container list, a poll timer) lives **in\n  the component**. That is what the phase's worked example already does\n  (`DockerProvider { service: DockerService }`, read by `perform`). A native provider holds a struct\n  field; a WASM plugin owns its own linear memory. The host is not involved and must not become so.\n- **State the retained tree binds to** is the gap. The tree lives host-side and a `ViewNode` is\n  static serializable data that **cannot carry a signal** (AGENTS.md ⭐ SETTLED). So a native\n  container flips a `Signal` and its `Label` repaints in place with no rebuild, while a plugin can\n  only re-send the tree. That asymmetry is what this closes.\n\n`AppState` (`heca/src/app_state.rs`, 77 fields) is explicitly **not** the target: it is host-only,\ncompiled, and unreachable from WASM. Nothing here adds a field to it.\n\n# What already exists — the pattern, twice, never generalised\n\n`SharedChromeState` (`heca/src/chrome/state.rs:700+`) already holds two lazily-created,\nstring-keyed signal maps:\n\n```rust\ncontainer_scroll: Rc<RefCell<HashMap<String, Signal<f32>>>>,   // state.rs:714\nkeyboard_target:  Rc<RefCell<HashMap<String, Signal<bool>>>>,  // state.rs:729\n```\n\nBoth share the properties this needs, and they are the template to copy:\n\n- **created on first ask** (`container_scroll()` at `state.rs:741`, `container_keyboard_target()` at\n  `state.rs:798`) — a mount does not have to be registered in advance;\n- **keyed by mount id**, not container kind — two placements of one container keep separate values,\n  the rule `kind()` vs `id()` sets (the phase's decision table, 2026-07-29);\n- they **outlive the retained tree**, so a rebuild (a pane's git status changing is enough) restores\n  rather than resets;\n- each has a **change-guarded setter that emits a `ChromeEvent`**\n  (`set_container_scroll` → `ContainerScrollChanged`, `state.rs:756`).\n\nWhat is missing is that each map is hardcoded for one concern the host already knew about. A Docker\ndock wanting \"is the daemon reachable\" has nowhere to put it.\n\nThe value type is **already built**: `PropValue` (`heca-view/src/lib.rs:580`) — serialisable scalars\n(`Bool`/`Int`/`Float`/`Text`/`Color`/`Glyph`/…) plus `List` and `Map`, made for exactly this\nboundary. Do not invent a second value vocabulary.\n\n# The work\n\n1. Add the map to `SharedChromeState` beside the two above, keyed by **`(mount, key)`**:\n   `container_values: Rc<RefCell<HashMap<(String, String), Signal<PropValue>>>>`.\n2. Two accessors on `SharedChromeState`, mirroring `container_scroll` / `set_container_scroll`\n   line for line (created on first ask; setter guarded, emits on a real change only):\n   ```rust\n   pub fn container_value(&self, mount: &str, key: &str) -> Signal<PropValue>;\n   pub fn set_container_value(&self, mount: &str, key: &str, value: PropValue);\n   ```\n   A first ask needs a default — take one (`container_value_or`) rather than inventing a null\n   variant; `PropValue` has no `Null` and must not grow one for this.\n3. One event: `ChromeEvent::ContainerValueChanged { container: String, key: String }`\n   (`heca/src/chrome/events.rs`), plus its `name()` arm — `\"container.value.changed\"`. Carry the key,\n   not the value: subscribers read through the signal, as they do for scroll.\n4. Expose both on `StateView` (`heca/src/host.rs:82+`), beside `container_scroll` /\n   `container_keyboard_target`, so a provider reaches them through the seam it already reads\n   through. The write side goes on `ProviderCx` (built by F003/P085/T353) so §2.3 still holds — a\n   provider never gets `&mut AppState`.\n5. `ViewNode` binding: a described `Label`/`Visibility`/`Badge` must be able to name a value key\n   instead of a literal, so the realized widget binds the signal and updates **in place**. This is\n   the half that makes the whole thing worth having — without it a plugin still rebuilds. Decide the\n   prop spelling with the user (a `PropValue::Text(\"@key\")` sigil is the cheap option and is\n   probably wrong — a distinct `PropValue::Binding(String)` says what it means and cannot collide\n   with a literal that happens to start with `@`).\n\n# Also here, because it is the same defect\n\n`pub workspaces: WorkspacesContainerState` (`heca/src/chrome/state.rs:702`) is a **hardcoded field\nfor the one built-in container**, and its own doc comment names the deadline: *\"Becomes a\ncontainer-id-keyed registry when a second container (Docker/agents/…) is added.\"* F003/P085/T359\nseated a second mount on 2026-07-30, so the deadline has passed. Fold it into the same keyed\nregistry, or say explicitly why the workspaces tree stays privileged — but do not leave the comment\ndescribing a future that has arrived.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- Two placements of one container: independent values, same as scroll and focus. A test with two\n  mount ids writing the same key must show two answers.\n- Lifetime: values die with the mount, like the dynamic-action handles in `ProviderHandles`\n  (`heca/src/providers/mod.rs:100`). An unmounted container must not leak its map entries.\n- A rebuild of the retained tree must not reset a value — the whole point of the signal living in\n  the store rather than in the widget.\n- The setter must stay change-guarded, or a plugin polling once a frame emits an event per frame.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- A provider can create, read and write a named value keyed by its mount, with no host code that\n  knows the key.\n- A `ViewNode`-described widget bound to a value updates **without a tree rebuild**.\n- `WorkspacesContainerState` is either keyed by container or explicitly justified as privileged.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings, and the USER drives the plugin case in the app —\n  a value changing on screen with no rebuild is a visual claim, not a unit-test one.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
